<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Social insurance</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1889">1889</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as old-age pensions</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Social insurance refers to state programmes that protect workers against risks such as sickness, accidents, old age and invalidity, funded by contributions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> introduced a pioneering system under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a> between 1883 and 1889, including health, accident and old-age pensions insurance. Accounts of the German Empire present it as an attempt to win workers' loyalty and undercut socialism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/social-insurance.jpg" alt="Social insurance" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yuasan · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OECD_Social_Security_Contributions.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
